experiments/2026-06-03/EXP-0010/committee1/evaluation_prosecutor.out:5:  - {name: "SnapKV / H2O", why: "State-of-the-art attention-based KV-cache eviction/compression that already retains high-attention tokens across long contexts. The proposed method's use of 'attention/citation signal' directly competes with this.", repo_or_cite: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.14469 (SnapKV) / https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14048 (H2O)"}
experiments/2026-06-03/EXP-0010/committee1/evaluation_prosecutor.out:15:  - Run a real multi-step tool-use benchmark (e.g., τ-bench) with a real LLM. Compare the proposed extractive compressor directly against SnapKV and LLMLingua at strictly matched context/KV token budgets, measuring end-to-end task success and empirical attention-to-future-reference predictive AUC.
experiments/2026-06-03/EXP-0010/committee1/evaluation_prosecutor.out:17:  - The evaluation is currently a circular synthetic exercise comparing against a toy baseline. You cannot claim an "attention/citation signal" compressor is effective without testing real attention on real tool outputs against the actual state-of-the-art in attention-based compression (SnapKV/H2O). The "make-or-break" negative (N3) is not refuted; it is merely hardcoded to succeed by the synthetic data generator.
experiments/2026-06-03/EXP-0010/committee1/theory_skeptic.out:27:    StreamingLLM/H2O/SnapKV operate on generic context.
experiments/2026-06-03/EXP-0010/committee1/theory_skeptic.out:28:  - No comparison against KV-eviction baselines (H2O, SnapKV) or prompt-compression
